%U A402445 757434521048050590528514511,2638732124527804054343898684055,9294302970348187046744271601348222191,182406716151767404661106468608340270883531
%N A402445 Number of distinct n X n blocks of visible and hidden trees in Euclid's orchard, where a tree stands at every point (x,y) with integer coordinates x,y >= 1 and the tree at (x,y) is visible from the origin if gcd(x,y) = 1 and hidden otherwise.
%C A402445 Write 1 for a visible tree and 0 for a hidden one: this gives the infinite binary array whose (x,y)-entry is 1 if gcd(x,y) = 1 and 0 otherwise, for x,y >= 1. An n X n block is a square piece of the array: the n^2 entries at the points (x+i, y+j) with 0 <= i,j < n, for some x,y >= 1. This is the block at (x,y), and (i,j) is the position of the entry at (x+i, y+j) inside the block. Two blocks are the same if they agree in all n^2 positions; blocks that differ by a rotation or a reflection are counted as different. The array is A054431 (the gcd array A003989 with every entry other than 1 replaced by 0). Visible from the origin means that the segment from (0,0) to the point (x,y) passes through no other point with integer coordinates (see the illustration in the Links section).
%C A402445 Which n X n patterns (arrays of 0's and 1's) occur as blocks is decided by a rule on remainders. For a prime p, sort the positions (i,j), 0 <= i,j < n, of a pattern into p^2 classes modulo p according to the remainders of i and j on division by p (for p = 2: i even or odd, j even or odd). A pattern passes the test for p if at least one class modulo p contains no 1; for p > n this is automatic, since some class is then empty. A pattern occurs as a block if and only if it passes the test for every prime p <= n (proved in the notes file). So a(n) is the number of n X n patterns that pass these tests: 15 of the 16 patterns for n = 2 and 377 of the 512 for n = 3 (see the Example section).
%C A402445 The rule on remainders and the terms a(2), a(3) and a(4) are in the literature. Kranakis and Pocchiola (1990, introduction) and Baake and Huck (2015, paragraph before Theorem 2) credit the rule to Herzog and Stewart (1971). Pleasants and Huck (2013, Theorem 2) prove it with x and y ranging over all integers (the proof in the notes file is for x,y >= 1), and they note in Section 4 that 135 of the 512 patterns for n = 3 do not occur. Murphy, Schmiedeler, and Stonner (2021, Tables 5.3-5.5) give 15, 377 and 14911 (their Table 3.1 has 50626 where Table 5.5 has 50625 for the 4 X 4 patterns that do not occur). The terms for n >= 5 were not found in the literature consulted.
%C A402445 Changing a 1 of a pattern to 0 cannot make the pattern fail a test. Hence changing any of the 1's of a block to 0's gives a block again (Pleasants and Huck, 2013, remark after Theorem 2; proved in the notes file); in particular the block of n^2 0's occurs for every n, and a(n) >= 2^m if some n X n block has m 1's. For n = 1..12 the largest number of 1's in an n X n block is 1, 3, 8, 12, 20, 24, 37, 45, 60, 68, 86, 94 (formula and proof in the notes file); for comparison, the block at (1,1) has A018805(n) 1's.
%C A402445 Every block occurs with positive frequency: for a given n X n block, the proportion of the pairs (x,y) with 1 <= x,y <= T at which it occurs tends to a positive limit as T -> oo (Pleasants and Huck, 2013, Theorems 1 and 2; proved in the notes file). The limit depends on the block: for n = 1 it is 6/Pi^2 = 0.6079271018... (A059956) for the block 1 and 1 - 6/Pi^2 for the block 0. The constant 6/Pi^2, the proportion of 1's in the array, also governs the growth of a(n) (see the Formula section); for comparison, log_2(a(n))/n^2 is still 0.67826... at n = 23.
%C A402445 If blocks differing only by a rotation or a reflection are counted once, the numbers for n = 1..6 are 2, 5, 77, 1948, 366250, 46763263 (n <= 23 in the notes file). If blocks are formed from every d-th row and column (the entries at (x+d*i, y+d*j), 0 <= i,j < n), then all 2^(n^2) patterns occur if and only if every prime p <= n divides d (proved in the notes file); the least such d is A034386(n), the product of the primes <= n. The one-dimensional analog is A278554, the number of distinct blocks of n consecutive terms of A008966, whose k-th term is 1 if k is squarefree (divisible by the square of no prime) and 0 otherwise. See the notes file in the Links section for proofs, sources and further data.
