%H A402445 Eric Weisstein's World of Mathematics, <a href="https://mathworld.wolfram.com/EuclidsOrchard.html">Euclid's Orchard</a>.
%F A402445 a(n) < 2^(n^2) for n >= 2, since the pattern of n^2 1's fails the test for p = 2 (patterns and tests are defined in the Comments section).
%F A402445 For primes q < p, the test for q implies the test for p (that is, every n X n pattern that passes the test for q also passes the test for p) if and only if n <= 2*p - q. With q = 2: the tests for p = 3, 5, 7 and 11 are implied by the test for p = 2 exactly for n <= 4, 8, 12 and 20, respectively. So it suffices to test p = 2 for n <= 4, p = 2 and 3 for n <= 8, and p = 2, 3 and 5 for n <= 12. For n = 13 the tests for p = 2, 3 and 5 do not suffice: some pattern passes them and fails the test for p = 7. (All proved in the notes file.)
%F A402445 log_2(a(n)) >= 6*n^2/Pi^2, and log_2(a(n)) = 6*n^2/Pi^2 + O(n^2/log(n)) as n -> oo (both proved in the notes file). Hence log_2(a(n))/n^2 tends to 6/Pi^2. This limit is due to Pleasants and Huck (2013, Theorem 3), who count the distinct pieces of the array cut out by disks instead of squares; by their note on p. 53 the limit is the same for squares.
%e A402445 The corner 1 <= x,y <= 8 of the array, with x increasing to the right and y upward:
%e A402445   y = 8:   1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0
%e A402445   y = 7:   1 1 1 1 1 1 0 1
%e A402445   y = 6:   1 0 0 0 1 0 1 0
%e A402445   y = 5:   1 1 1 1 0 1 1 1
%e A402445   y = 4:   1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0
%e A402445   y = 3:   1 1 0 1 1 0 1 1
%e A402445   y = 2:   1 0 1 0 1 0 1 0
%e A402445   y = 1:   1 1 1 1 1 1 1 1
%e A402445   x =      1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8
%e A402445 Blocks and patterns are written row by row from the top row (largest y) to the bottom row, with a slash between the rows. The 2 X 2 block at (1,1) is 10/11. The 3 X 3 block at (20,25) is 101/010/011, since the values of gcd(x,y) for x = 20, 21, 22 are 1, 3, 1 for y = 27, then 2, 1, 2 for y = 26 and 5, 1, 1 for y = 25 (this block is outlined in the illustration in the Links section, which shows the 1's as black cells and calls the blocks pictures).
%e A402445 In the block at (20,25) the positions (i,j) for which 20+i and 25+j are both even are (0,1) and (2,1). They form one of the four classes modulo 2 (i even, j odd), and the entries there are 0, so the block passes the test for p = 2. In the same way, in the block at any (x,y) and for every prime p <= n, the positions (i,j) for which p divides both x+i and y+j form a class modulo p, and the entries there are 0. This is why every block passes every test. The converse needs the Chinese remainder theorem (see the notes file).
%e A402445 For n = 1 the block 1 occurs at (1,1) and the block 0 at (2,2), so a(1) = 2.
%e A402445 For n = 2 each of the four positions is a class modulo 2 by itself, so the only pattern that fails the test for p = 2 is the one with four 1's; it cannot occur because one of x, x+1 is even and one of y, y+1 is even, and the entry at the point with these coordinates is 0. The other fifteen patterns occur as blocks by the rule in the Comments section, so a(2) = 15:
%e A402445   00/00
%e A402445   10/00  01/00  00/10  00/01
%e A402445   11/00  10/10  10/01  01/10  01/01  00/11
%e A402445   11/10  11/01  10/11  01/11
%e A402445 For n = 3 the four classes modulo 2 consist of the positions marked A, B, C and D (i increases to the right and j upward):
%e A402445   A B A
%e A402445   C D C
%e A402445   A B A
%e A402445 They have 4, 2, 2 and 1 positions. A pattern fails the test for p = 2 if and only if it has a 1 in each of the four classes, which happens for (2^4-1)*(2^2-1)*(2^2-1)*(2^1-1) = 135 patterns. Modulo 3 each of the nine positions is a class by itself, so the test for p = 3 asks for at least one 0, and every pattern that passes the test for p = 2 has one. Hence a(3) = 2^9 - 135 = 377.
%e A402445 For n = 4 each class modulo 2 has four positions, so (2^4-1)^4 = 50625 patterns fail the test for p = 2. The test for p = 3 is again implied by the test for p = 2: each class modulo 2 contains one of the positions (1,1), (1,2), (2,1), (2,2), and each of these is a class modulo 3 by itself. Hence a(4) = 2^16 - 50625 = 14911.
%e A402445 For n = 5 the classes modulo 2 have 9, 6, 6 and 4 positions, so 2^25 - (2^9-1)*(2^6-1)^2*(2^4-1) = 3132047 patterns pass the test for p = 2. Of these, 236161 fail the test for p = 3, which is no longer implied (the notes file counts them by inclusion-exclusion over the classes modulo 2 that contain no 1); the test for p = 5 is implied by the test for p = 2 (see the Formula section). Hence a(5) = 3132047 - 236161 = 2895886.
%o A402445 (Python)
%o A402445 from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor
%o A402445 from math import gcd
%o A402445 from os import cpu_count
%o A402445 b = 4233
%o A402445 def scan(bounds):
%o A402445     lo, hi = bounds
%o A402445     rows = [[int(gcd(x, y) == 1) for y in range(1, b+3)] for x in (lo, lo+1)]
%o A402445     seen = [set(), set(), set()]
%o A402445     for x in range(lo, hi+1):
%o A402445         rows.append([int(gcd(x+2, y) == 1) for y in range(1, b+3)])
%o A402445         p = q = 0
%o A402445         for j in range(b+2):
%o A402445             v = rows[0][j] + 2*rows[1][j] + 4*rows[2][j]
%o A402445             p, q = (p >> 2) | ((v & 3) << 2), (q >> 3) | (v << 6)
%o A402445             if j < b: seen[0].add(v & 1)
%o A402445             if 1 <= j <= b: seen[1].add(p)
%o A402445             if j >= 2: seen[2].add(q)
%o A402445         rows.pop(0)
%o A402445     return seen
%o A402445 if __name__ == '__main__':
%o A402445     w = cpu_count() or 1
%o A402445     with ProcessPoolExecutor(w) as pool:
%o A402445         runs = list(pool.map(scan, [(1+b*t//w, b*(t+1)//w) for t in range(w)]))
%o A402445     for n in range(3): print(len(set().union(*(r[n] for r in runs))))
%o A402445 (Rust) // See Links section.
